// 英语母语的用户学日语：今日一句的英文版（app.js 按日语原文换上）
// 自动生成：python3 tools/ennat/build.py（编辑 tools/ennat/out/*.json）
const EN_JA_DAILY = {"あけましておめでとうございます":["Happy New Year","The set greeting used the first time you see someone in the new year or when writing New Year's cards (年賀状). It is normally used only during the first days of the year (松の内), and in casual speech it is often shortened to 「あけおめ」.","Happy New Year! I look forward to another year of your kindness and support.","season"],"初夢":["first dream of the New Year","The first dream of the new year, generally the one you have between the night of New Year's Day and the night of January 2. Tradition says the luckiest things to dream of are, in order, Mt. Fuji (富士), a hawk (鷹), and an eggplant (茄子).","My first dream of the year was about Mt. Fuji, so it looks like it's going to be a good year.","season"],"今年もよろしくお願いします":["I look forward to your continued support this year","The line that goes hand in hand with 「あけましておめでとうございます」 in New Year's greetings. You can say it to coworkers, friends, and neighbors alike.","Happy New Year, senpai! I hope I can count on you again this year.","season"],"少年老い易く学成り難し":["Youth fades fast, and learning comes slowly","It means that people grow old quickly while real learning is hard to achieve. So value your time and study hard while you can.","Youth fades fast, and learning comes slowly. Study hard while you're young.","proverb"],"小寒":["Lesser Cold (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms of the traditional calendar, falling around January 5. It marks 「寒の入り」, the start of the coldest period of the year.","Today is Lesser Cold, so the coldest season is finally here.","season"],"無理難題":["unreasonable demand; impossible request","A demand that is unreasonable and simply cannot be met, often used in 「無理難題を押し付ける」 (to force an impossible demand on someone). It is stronger than just 'a difficult problem': the point is that the request itself is unfair.","My boss keeps forcing impossible demands on me, and I don't know what to do.","four-character idiom"],"七草粥":["seven-herb rice porridge","Rice porridge eaten on the morning of January 7, cooked with the 「春の七草」 (seven herbs of spring) such as water dropwort (芹) and shepherd's purse (薺). It is a wish for a year free of illness, and it also gives a stomach worn out by New Year's feasting a rest.","After all the New Year's food, seven-herb porridge is gentle on a tired stomach.","season"],"手も足も出ない":["to be helpless; to not stand a chance","Literally 'can't put out a hand or a foot,' it means being completely powerless. It's used when an opponent or a problem is simply too much for you.","The other team was too strong; we didn't stand a chance.","idiom"],"自分のペースでいいよ":["Go at your own pace","Tells someone they don't have to compare themselves with others and can move forward at their own pace. 「いいよ」 gives it a gentle, reassuring tone.","You don't have to compare yourself with other people. Just go at your own pace.","kind words"],"出る杭は打たれる":["The nail that sticks out gets hammered down","Literally 'the stake that sticks out gets hit,' it means people who stand out too much tend to attract envy and get pushed back down. It's close to the idea of 'tall poppy syndrome' in English.","They say the nail that sticks out gets hammered down, but I still want to state my opinions clearly.","proverb"],"鏡開き":["kagami-biraki (breaking open the New Year's rice cake)","The custom of breaking up and eating the round rice cakes (鏡餅) offered over the New Year, usually on January 11. Because 「切る」 (to cut) and 「割る」 (to split) are considered unlucky words, the auspicious 「開く」 (to open) is used instead.","I made oshiruko (sweet red bean soup) with the rice cake from kagami-biraki.","season"],"無我夢中":["totally absorbed; lost in the moment","Describes being so focused that you forget yourself, often while doing something with all your might. In English you might say 'lose yourself in something' or 'be in the zone.'","I ran with everything I had, and the next thing I knew I'd crossed the finish line.","four-character idiom"],"歯を食いしばる":["to grit one's teeth","Means enduring pain or hanging on with all your strength. English uses exactly the same image: 'to grit your teeth.'","I gritted my teeth and ran all the way to the end.","idiom"],"お疲れ様":["Thanks for your hard work; good work","The most common phrase of appreciation in Japan, said when leaving work, finishing a task, or simply passing a coworker. To superiors, use the more polite 「お疲れ様です」 or 「お疲れ様でした」.","Thanks for all your hard work today. Get some good rest.","kind words"],"一を聞いて十を知る":["Hear one, understand ten; to grasp things instantly","From the Analects of Confucius, it describes a very bright person who understands things quickly. Hearing just one part, they grasp the whole.","She's the type who hears one thing and understands ten, so explanations get through to her right away.","proverb"],"一騎当千":["a match for a thousand","Literally 'one horseman can hold off a thousand.' It describes someone whose strength or skill far surpasses others.","They're all veteran players, each one a match for a thousand.","four-character idiom"],"頭が固い":["stubborn; inflexible","Literally 'one's head is hard,' it describes someone who is rigid and can't adapt their thinking. It's similar to English 'set in one's ways.'","My grandfather is set in his ways and won't accept anything new.","idiom"],"えらいね":["Good for you! Well done!","Praise for someone who has done something commendable. It's often used to praise children, but friends also say it to give each other credit.","You get up early every morning to study? Good for you!","kind words"],"壁に耳あり障子に目あり":["The walls have ears","Literally 'walls have ears, and paper screens have eyes,' it means secrets leak easily, so be careful what you say. English has the same saying: 'The walls have ears.'","The walls have ears, so let's not talk about secrets here.","proverb"],"大寒":["Greater Cold (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around January 20, and the coldest time of the year. Japanese people traditionally use this time for 「寒稽古」 (midwinter training), brewing sake, and making miso.","On the morning of Greater Cold, the windows were completely covered in frost.","season"],"頭寒足熱":["cool head, warm feet (for good health)","A traditional Japanese health saying: keeping your head cool and your feet warm is good for your health and sleep. The four characters literally mean 'head cold, feet hot.'","I try to keep my head cool and my feet warm, so I soak my feet in hot water before bed.","four-character idiom"],"棚に上げる":["to ignore one's own faults (while criticizing others)","Literally 'to put something up on a shelf,' it means conveniently keeping quiet about your own shortcomings while criticizing others. It often appears as 「自分のことは棚に上げて」.","He ignores his own faults and does nothing but complain about other people.","idiom"],"おかえりなさい":["Welcome home","What you say to welcome a family member home; the person coming home says 「ただいま」. It can also be used to welcome back a friend who has been away for a long time.","Welcome home. Dinner's ready.","kind words"],"言うは易く行うは難し":["Easier said than done","It means something is easy to say but hard to actually do. It matches the English proverb 'Easier said than done' exactly.","I decided to study every day, but it's easier said than done.","proverb"],"異口同音":["with one voice; in unison","Describes many people saying the same thing or being in complete agreement. The characters literally mean 'different mouths, same sound.'","The participants all said with one voice, 'That was fun!'","four-character idiom"],"首にする":["to fire (someone)","Literally 'to make it the neck,' evoking a beheading; in Japanese it means to dismiss an employee. Being fired is 「首になる」, much like English 'to get the axe.'","I was late all the time, and I got fired.","idiom"],"お元気で":["Take care; all the best","A parting wish used when saying goodbye to someone for a long time, hoping they stay healthy and well. It's like English 'Take care' or 'All the best.'","Take care over there. Let's meet again.","kind words"],"百聞は一見に如かず":["Seeing is believing","It means seeing something once with your own eyes is better than hearing about it a hundred times. It comes from the ancient Chinese history Book of Han, and English expresses the same idea with 'Seeing is believing.'","Seeing is believing. Why not visit Kyoto and see it for yourself?","proverb"],"油断大敵":["Carelessness is your worst enemy","「油断」 is a Japanese word meaning carelessness or letting your guard down; despite its kanji ('oil' + 'cut off'), it has nothing to do with oil. Close English equivalents are 'Never let your guard down' and 'Pride comes before a fall.'","They may be a weak team, but don't let your guard down!","four-character idiom"],"顔に泥を塗る":["to bring shame on someone; to make someone lose face","Literally 'to smear mud on someone's face,' it means causing someone, especially a parent or superior, to lose face. English has a similar image in 'to drag someone's name through the mud.'","Don't do anything that would bring shame on your parents.","idiom"],"頑張りすぎないで":["Don't push yourself too hard","A caring thing to say to someone who is already working very hard. It's gentler than just saying 「頑張って」 and reminds them to take care of themselves.","You're already working hard enough, so don't overdo it, OK?","kind words"],"下手な鉄砲も数撃ちゃ当たる":["Fire enough shots and one is bound to hit","Literally 'even a poor marksman will hit something if he fires enough shots,' it means that if you keep trying, you'll succeed sooner or later. It's similar to English 'If at first you don't succeed, try, try again.'","Even a bad shot hits the target if he fires enough, so I applied to all kinds of companies.","proverb"],"意気投合":["to hit it off; to click","Describes people whose feelings and ideas match so they get along great, often right from the first meeting. The English equivalent is 'to hit it off.'","Even though it was the first time they'd met, the two of them hit it off right away.","four-character idiom"],"節分":["Setsubun (the day before the start of spring)","The day before 立春. Families scatter roasted beans while shouting 「鬼は外」 (demons out) and 「福は内」 (good luck in) to drive away evil, and there's also a custom of eating 「恵方巻」 sushi rolls while facing that year's lucky direction.","On the night of Setsubun, I threw beans with my kids, shouting 'Demons out, good luck in!'","season"],"立春":["Beginning of Spring (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around February 4. On the calendar, spring begins on this day, although in reality it's still quite cold.","According to the calendar it's the beginning of spring, but the cold days just keep on coming.","season"],"目を通す":["to look over; to skim through","Means to read through documents or materials quickly to get the gist. It's a very common expression at work.","Please look over the materials before the meeting.","idiom"],"いつもありがとう":["Thanks for everything; thank you, as always","Thanks for the everyday things your family, friends, or coworkers do for you. People don't often say it out loud, so hearing it can really move someone.","Mom, thanks for everything you always do.","kind words"],"余寒お見舞い申し上げます":["Wishing you well in the lingering cold","A seasonal greeting for letters or cards sent after 立春, asking after the recipient's health during the late winter chill. Before 立春, 「寒中お見舞い申し上げます」 is used instead.","Wishing you well in the lingering cold. The cold weather is set to continue, so please take good care of yourself.","season"],"一寸の虫にも五分の魂":["Even the smallest creature has its pride","Literally 'even a one-inch bug has a half-inch soul,' it means that even small, weak people have their own spirit and dignity and shouldn't be looked down on. Compare English 'Even a worm will turn.'","Even the smallest bug has a soul. I'd appreciate it if you didn't look down on me just because I'm new.","proverb"],"用意周到":["thoroughly prepared; careful planning","Describes someone who has prepared carefully for every possibility. 「用意」 means 'preparation' and 「周到」 means 'thorough' or 'meticulous.'","She's so well prepared that she even brought two umbrellas.","four-character idiom"],"風花":["snow flurries from a clear sky","Fine snowflakes drifting down like flower petals from a clear sky, usually snow blown in from distant mountains. It's a beautiful winter word.","Even though it's sunny, light flakes of snow are fluttering through the air.","season"],"油を売る":["to goof off; to slack off chatting","It's said that in the Edo period, hair-oil sellers chatted with customers while slowly pouring out the oil. From this, it came to mean idling and chatting during work time.","Where have you been goofing off?","idiom"],"ちゃんと見てるよ":["I see how hard you're trying","Lets someone know their efforts have been noticed and recognized, which is very reassuring. 「ちゃんと」 means 'properly' or 'really,' as in 'I really am watching.'","I see how hard you work every single day.","kind words"],"人の振り見て我が振り直せ":["Learn from others' faults and correct your own","It means watching how others behave and using it to reflect on and correct your own conduct. It's close to a line from the Analects of Confucius: 'When you see someone unworthy, look inward and examine yourself.'","Seeing people making a racket on the train, I thought to myself: learn from others' faults and correct your own.","proverb"],"バレンタインデー":["Valentine's Day","In Japan it's mainly a day when women give chocolate. There's 「本命チョコ」 for the person they love, 「義理チョコ」 ('obligation chocolate') for coworkers, and 「友チョコ」 for friends.","On Valentine's Day, I swapped 'friend chocolates' with my friends.","season"],"不言実行":["actions, not words","A four-character idiom coined in Japan, meaning to quietly get things done without a lot of talk. The English equivalent is 'Actions speak louder than words.'","My father is a man of action, not words; he works without a single complaint.","four-character idiom"],"肩を落とす":["to be dejected; shoulders slumped","Literally 'to drop one's shoulders,' it describes looking disappointed or discouraged. English uses the same image: 'slumped shoulders.'","After losing the game, the players' shoulders slumped in disappointment.","idiom"],"一緒に頑張ろう":["Let's do our best together","Means you'll work hard side by side with the other person. It feels more supportive than just saying 「頑張って」, because you're in it together.","There's one month left until the exam. Let's do our best together.","kind words"],"井の中の蛙大海を知らず":["A frog in a well knows nothing of the great ocean","It describes someone with a narrow outlook who doesn't know the wider world. It comes from the ancient Chinese classic Zhuangzi.","Studying abroad made me realize I had been a frog in a well that knew nothing of the ocean.","proverb"],"雨水":["Rain Water (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around February 19, when snow gradually turns to rain and ice begins to melt. Traditionally it's considered a good time to set out the dolls for the Girls' Festival.","Now that Rain Water has passed, the snow has started turning to rain.","season"],"馬耳東風":["in one ear and out the other","From a line by the Chinese poet Li Bai about the east wind blowing past a horse's ear, it means paying no attention to other people's advice. In English you'd say it 'goes in one ear and out the other.'","No matter how many times I warn my little brother, it goes in one ear and out the other.","four-character idiom"],"顔から火が出る":["to blush with embarrassment","Literally 'fire comes out of one's face,' it describes being terribly embarrassed. It's like saying 'my face was burning' in English.","I tripped in front of everyone, and I was so embarrassed my face was burning.","idiom"],"成長したね":["You've really grown","Expresses how much someone has improved or matured compared with before. It's a way of recognizing the effort they've put in.","You speak much more confidently than before. You've really grown.","kind words"],"縁の下の力持ち":["an unsung hero; a pillar behind the scenes","Literally 'the strong man under the floorboards,' it describes someone who quietly works behind the scenes supporting others. The English equivalent is 'an unsung hero.'","The team manager is the unsung hero of our team.","proverb"],"春一番":["the first strong southerly wind of spring","The first strong south wind to blow between 立春 and 春分. It's announced by the Japan Meteorological Agency and means spring is on its way.","The first spring gale blew today, and it suddenly got much warmer.","season"],"七転八倒":["writhing in agony","Describes being in extreme pain and twisting and turning in agony. Don't confuse it with the similar-looking 七転八起, which means 'fall down seven times, get up eight.'","In the middle of the night, I was writhing in agony with a terrible stomachache.","four-character idiom"],"息が合う":["to be in sync; to work well together","Literally 'breaths match,' it means doing something together in perfect harmony and timing. In English we'd say people are 'in sync.'","The two of them are perfectly in sync when they dance.","idiom"],"元気をもらった":["You gave me energy; you lifted my spirits","Literally 'I received energy,' it means you were encouraged by someone's words, smile, or performance. It's a very common way for Japanese people to say thank you.","Your smile really lifted my spirits.","kind words"],"過ぎたるは猶及ばざるが如し":["Too much is as bad as too little","From the Analects of Confucius, it means overdoing something is just as bad as not doing enough. English has a similar saying: 'Everything in moderation.'","There's no point studying so hard that you wreck your health. Too much is as bad as too little.","proverb"],"日進月歩":["rapid, constant progress","Literally 'advancing by the day, stepping forward by the month.' It describes steady, rapid progress and is often used about science and technology.","IT is advancing by leaps and bounds.","four-character idiom"],"白羽の矢が立つ":["to be singled out; to be chosen","According to legend, when a god chose someone as an offering, a white-feathered arrow would be stuck in the roof of that person's house. Today it usually means being picked for an important role.","She was singled out to lead the new project.","idiom"],"一人じゃないよ":["You're not alone","Said to someone who feels lonely or is going through a hard time. It reminds them that you and others are right there with them.","Lean on me when things get tough. You're not alone.","kind words"],"ひな祭り":["Hinamatsuri, the Girls' Festival","A festival on March 3 to pray for girls' healthy growth. Families display hina dolls and enjoy chirashi sushi, diamond-shaped rice cakes (菱餅), and 白酒 (a sweet rice drink); it's also called 「桃の節句」, the Peach Festival.","For Hinamatsuri, I made chirashi sushi for my daughter.","season"],"若い時の苦労は買ってでもせよ":["Hardship in youth is worth paying for","It means the hardships you go through when young become a treasure later in life, so they're worth seeking out even if you had to pay for them. Literally 'buy hardship in your youth if you have to.'","They say hardship in your youth is worth paying for, so I'm going to give my part-time job my all too.","proverb"],"啓蟄":["Awakening of Insects (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around March 5, when hibernating insects start coming out of the ground. The name itself means 'insects awakening,' a vivid sign of spring.","Since the Awakening of Insects, I've started seeing bugs in the garden.","season"],"一喜一憂":["to swing between joy and worry","Describes being happy one moment and anxious the next as a situation changes. It's often used to advise people not to get too caught up in short-term results.","Don't get too up or down about your test scores; what matters is sticking with it.","four-character idiom"],"手を貸す":["to lend a hand","Literally 'to lend one's hand.' It means helping someone, just like the English expression 'lend a hand.'","My bags are heavy. Could you give me a hand?","idiom"],"肩の力を抜いて":["Relax; loosen up","Literally 'release the strength from your shoulders,' it means don't be so tense or try so hard, and just face things calmly. English similarly says 'loosen up.'","When it's the real thing, just relax and do it the way you always do.","kind words"],"人事を尽くして天命を待つ":["Do your best and leave the rest to fate","It means doing everything in your power and then leaving the outcome to heaven. English has a similar idea in 'Do your best and let God do the rest.'","I've done all the preparation. I've done my part, and now the rest is up to fate.","proverb"],"適材適所":["the right person in the right place","Means assigning people to the positions that best suit their abilities. The English equivalent is 'the right person for the right job.'","Putting the right people in the right roles improved the team's results.","four-character idiom"],"胡麻をする":["to butter someone up; to suck up","Literally 'to grind sesame seeds.' When you grind sesame in a mortar, the seeds stick all around the sides, so it came to mean flattering everyone to win favor.","He's trying to get ahead by sucking up to his boss.","idiom"],"なんくるないさ":["It'll all work out","An Okinawan dialect phrase with a meaning close to 「なんとかなる」. It's often cited as an expression of the optimistic, easygoing outlook of Okinawan people.","You worry too much. It'll all work out.","kind words"],"青は藍より出でて藍より青し":["The student surpasses the master","From the ancient Chinese classic Xunzi: blue dye comes from the indigo plant, yet it is bluer than the plant itself. It describes a student who outshines their teacher.","The apprentice has surpassed his master. Blue comes from indigo, yet is bluer than indigo.","proverb"],"ホワイトデー":["White Day","A holiday that started in Japan, when men give return gifts to the women who gave them chocolate on Valentine's Day. Popular gifts include candy and cookies.","I still can't decide what to give as a return gift for White Day.","season"],"創意工夫":["creativity and ingenuity","「創意」 means original ideas, and 「工夫」 means figuring out a clever way to do something or putting thought into it. Together they describe coming up with inventive solutions.","Even with only a few ingredients, a little creativity lets you make a delicious meal.","four-character idiom"],"足を洗う":["to wash one's hands of something; to go straight","Literally 'to wash one's feet,' it means quitting a shady line of work or leaving a bad crowd. English says 'to wash one's hands of something,' but Japanese washes the feet.","He cut ties with his bad friends and went straight.","idiom"],"失敗してもいいんだよ":["It's okay to fail","Encourages someone to give things a bold try without being afraid of failure. The 「んだよ」 ending adds a gentle, reassuring tone.","Everyone's bad at it in the beginning, so it's okay to fail.","kind words"],"お彼岸":["Ohigan (the equinox weeks for visiting graves)","The seven days centered on the spring and autumn equinoxes. During this time Japanese people visit family graves and offer 「ぼたもち」 (also called 「おはぎ」, rice balls coated in sweet red bean paste) to their ancestors.","Since it's Ohigan, the whole family went to visit the family grave.","season"],"嘘から出たまこと":["a lie that comes true","It means something that started as a lie or a joke ends up becoming reality. English has a related saying: 'Many a true word is spoken in jest.'","I joked about studying abroad, and then the joke actually came true.","proverb"],"春分":["Spring Equinox (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around March 20, when day and night are almost equal in length. 「春分の日」 (Vernal Equinox Day) is a national holiday in Japan.","After the spring equinox, the days keep getting longer.","season"],"老若男女":["people of all ages and both sexes","The reading is irregular: 若 is read にゃく and 女 is read にょ. Note the order, too: 'old, young, men, women,' whereas English usually says 'men and women, young and old.'","People of all ages, young and old, men and women, come to this festival.","four-character idiom"],"気が利く":["thoughtful; attentive","Describes someone who notices little things and thinks of others' needs before being asked. It's a compliment you'll often hear about coworkers or friends.","He's very attentive, so he's popular at work.","idiom"],"いい一日を":["Have a nice day","A parting wish for when you say goodbye in the morning or leave someone a message. It's short for 「いい一日をお過ごしください」, just like English 'Have a nice day.'","It's a beautiful sunny day. Have a good one!","kind words"],"ご卒業おめでとうございます":["Congratulations on your graduation","Graduation ceremonies in Japan are usually held in March, and this is the standard way to congratulate someone. With friends, you can simply say 「卒業おめでとう」.","Congratulations on your graduation. I wish you every success on your new path.","season"],"習うより慣れろ":["Practice makes perfect","Literally 'get used to it rather than learn it,' it means repeated hands-on practice teaches you more than listening to explanations. English says 'Practice makes perfect.'","With conversation, practice makes perfect, so talk with Japanese people as much as you can.","proverb"],"粉骨砕身":["to work oneself to the bone; to give one's all","Literally 'grinding one's bones and crushing one's body,' it's used in Japanese to express determination, for example in a speech when starting a new job. Despite the violent image, it isn't about sacrifice or a terrible fate; it simply means 'I'll give it everything I've got.'","I will devote myself body and soul to my new workplace.","four-character idiom"],"歯が立たない":["to be no match for; to be out of one's depth","Literally 'one's teeth can't bite into it,' it means an opponent is too strong or a problem too hard to deal with. Compare English 'a tough nut to crack.'","This problem is way too hard. I can't make a dent in it.","idiom"],"桜前線":["the cherry blossom front","A line connecting places where the cherry trees bloom on the same day. Like a weather front on a map, it moves from south to north.","The cherry blossom front has moved north, and the cherry trees have started blooming in Tokyo too.","season"],"諦めたらそこで試合終了ですよ":["If you give up, the game is over","A famous line spoken by Coach Anzai in Takehiko Inoue's basketball manga SLAM DUNK. It encourages people to keep going until the very end.","There's still time. If you give up, that's when the game ends.","kind words"],"実るほど頭を垂れる稲穂かな":["The fuller the ear of rice, the lower it bows","Just as ripe ears of rice bend low, the more learned and cultivated a person is, the more humble they become. It's used to praise people who stay modest despite their achievements.","That professor is so distinguished, yet so humble. The fuller the ear of rice, the lower it bows.","proverb"],"文武両道":["excelling in both academics and sports","Originally it meant mastery of both scholarship and the martial arts. In modern Japanese it usually describes someone who is good at both studying and sports.","He excels at both academics and sports: he gets good grades and is the ace of the soccer team.","four-character idiom"],"新年度":["the new fiscal or school year","In Japan, most schools, companies, and government offices start their year on April 1. That's why school entrance, new jobs, and staff transfers all happen at this time.","The new fiscal year has started, and some new people have joined our workplace.","season"],"腰が低い":["humble; modest","Literally 'one's waist is low,' as in bowing deeply, it describes someone who is modest and doesn't act self-important. It's a compliment.","He's the company president, yet he's a very humble person.","idiom"],"ドンマイ":["Don't worry about it; never mind","Japanese-made English from 'don't mind,' often said to a teammate who makes a mistake in sports. Today it's also very common in everyday life.","Don't worry about it. We'll get it next time!","kind words"],"清明":["Clear and Bright (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around April 4. Its name comes from 「清浄明潔」 (pure and bright), as everything feels fresh and clear. Unlike China, where this is the tomb-sweeping festival, Japan generally has no custom of visiting graves at this time.","Around Clear and Bright, the sky and the flowers are so bright that it really lifts your mood.","season"],"花見":["hanami, cherry blossom viewing","People spread out mats under the cherry trees and eat, drink, and chat with family, friends, or coworkers while admiring the blossoms. It's one of the most important spring events in Japan.","This weekend I'm going cherry blossom viewing in Ueno Park with my coworkers.","season"],"学問に王道なし":["There is no royal road to learning","From the Western proverb 'There is no royal road to learning.' It means there are no easy shortcuts to knowledge; you simply have to put in steady effort.","There's no royal road to learning. You just have to memorize things one at a time.","proverb"],"ご入学おめでとうございます":["Congratulations on starting school","Entrance ceremonies in Japan are mostly held in early April. This is the standard phrase for congratulating someone, or their child, on starting school.","Congratulations on your child starting school. They look adorable with their new school backpack!","season"],"千載一遇":["a once-in-a-lifetime chance","Literally 'a meeting that comes once in a thousand years.' It refers to a golden opportunity that hardly ever comes along.","This is the chance of a lifetime.","four-character idiom"],"耳にたこができる":["to be sick of hearing something","Here 「たこ」 means a callus, not an octopus. It describes hearing the same thing so many times that you're fed up with it, as if your ears had grown calluses.","I've heard my mom's nagging so many times I'm sick of it.","idiom"],"花冷え":["a cold snap during cherry blossom season","A sudden return of cold weather while the cherry trees are in full bloom. It's a seasonal word with a very Japanese feel.","These chilly blossom-season days keep coming, so I still can't put my coat away.","season"],"楽しみにしてるね":["I'm looking forward to it","Shows that you're looking forward to seeing someone or to what they'll do. Hearing it makes people feel valued.","I'm really looking forward to seeing you next month.","kind words"],"楽あれば苦あり":["No pleasure without pain","It means that life brings pain along with pleasure, and the two always take turns. It's often paired with 「苦あれば楽あり」 (after hardship comes ease).","No pleasure without pain: after my trip, my homework has piled up like a mountain.","proverb"],"一念発起":["to resolve to do something; to turn over a new leaf","Originally a Buddhist term for resolving to devote oneself to the Buddhist path. Now it means making up your mind to start something new.","I made up my mind and started jogging every morning.","four-character idiom"],"花吹雪":["a blizzard of cherry petals","The sight of cherry blossom petals swirling down in the wind like a snowstorm. 「吹雪」 by itself means 'blizzard.'","Every time the wind blew, the petals swirled around like snow. It was so beautiful.","season"],"根掘り葉掘り":["to pry into every detail","Literally 'digging up the roots and the leaves.' It describes questioning someone about every last detail, usually to an annoying degree.","I got grilled about every little detail of my love life.","idiom"],"ほっとした":["What a relief; I'm relieved","The feeling of sudden relief when something you were worried about turns out fine. 「ほっと」 is a mimetic word for breathing a sigh of relief.","When I heard I'd passed the exam, I was so relieved.","kind words"],"石の上にも三年":["Perseverance pays off","Literally 'even a cold stone gets warm if you sit on it for three years,' it means that if you endure and keep at it, you'll succeed in the end. Compare English 'Rome wasn't built in a day.'","My new job is tough, but I'll stick with it, telling myself that perseverance pays off.","proverb"],"我田引水":["self-serving; drawing water to one's own field","Literally 'drawing water into one's own rice field,' it means thinking only of your own benefit or twisting facts to suit yourself. It's a four-character idiom created in Japan.","His opinion is completely self-serving; he isn't thinking about the big picture.","four-character idiom"],"頭に来る":["to get mad; to tick someone off","A casual way of saying you're really angry, as if the anger has rushed to your head. It's similar to English 'to blow one's top.'","No matter how many times I told him, he wouldn't listen, so I got really mad.","idiom"],"穀雨":["Grain Rain (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around April 20, when spring rains nourish all the grains. It's the last solar term of spring.","The rain around Grain Rain is a blessing for the fields.","season"],"ひと休みしよう":["Let's take a break","A suggestion to stop for a short rest, often used in the middle of work or studying. 「ひと」 means 'one' here, so it's literally 'let's have one rest.'","We've been sitting forever. Let's take a break.","kind words"],"果報は寝て待て":["Good things come to those who wait","It means you can't force good fortune; once you've done your best, wait calmly for the result. Literally 'wait for good luck while sleeping.'","I've done all I can, so now I'll just sit back and wait for good news.","proverb"],"和洋折衷":["a blend of Japanese and Western styles","Describes a fusion of Japanese and Western styles. It's often used for architecture, food, and clothing.","This inn is built in a mix of Japanese and Western styles, and it's very relaxing.","four-character idiom"],"口が軽い":["loose-lipped; can't keep a secret","Literally 'one's mouth is light,' it describes someone who can't keep a secret. The opposite is 「口が堅い」.","You'd better not tell your secrets to people with loose lips.","idiom"],"深呼吸しよう":["Let's take a deep breath","Said to calm yourself or someone else down when nervous, flustered, or angry. It's a simple way to help someone get back to normal.","Nervous? First, take a deep breath.","kind words"],"後悔先に立たず":["There's no use regretting it afterward","Literally 'regret never comes first,' it means that regretting things after the fact does no good, so think carefully beforehand. English has a similar warning: 'Don't shut the barn door after the horse has bolted.'","Regret always comes too late. Let's review properly before the exam.","proverb"],"優柔不断":["indecisive; wishy-washy","Describes someone who wavers and can't make up their mind. In English you might call such a person 'wishy-washy.'","I'm so indecisive that it takes me forever to choose from a menu.","four-character idiom"],"腕が上がる":["to improve one's skills","In Japanese, 「腕」 (arm) stands for skill or ability, so 「腕が上がる」 means your skills have improved. It can also mean that you can hold more alcohol than before.","I've practiced every day, and my cooking has gotten better.","idiom"],"いただきます":["Itadakimasu (said before eating)","Said before a meal, often with palms pressed together. It expresses gratitude to the food, to the person who made it, and to nature's bounty.","Is everyone here? All right then, let's eat!","kind words"],"柳に雪折れなし":["Supple willows don't break under snow","Literally 'willow branches don't snap under snow,' it means that soft, flexible things can withstand hardship better than rigid ones. English has a similar proverb: 'Better to bend than to break.'","Like the willow that never snaps under the snow, if you think flexibly you can get through hard times.","proverb"],"針小棒大":["making a mountain out of a molehill","Literally 'turning something as small as a needle into something as big as a pole,' it means wildly exaggerating. It's a four-character idiom created in Japan, and the English equivalent is 'making a mountain out of a molehill.'","She always blows things out of proportion, so let's only believe half of what she says.","four-character idiom"],"八十八夜":["the 88th night (tea-picking season)","The 88th day counted from 立春, usually around May 2. It's the season for picking the year's first tea, and folk belief says tea picked on this day brings good health.","I was given some new tea picked on the 88th night.","season"],"気が短い":["short-tempered; impatient","Literally 'one's temper is short,' it describes someone who gets irritated or angry easily. English likewise says 'short-tempered.'","My dad is short-tempered, so he gets angry if you keep him waiting.","idiom"],"どういたしまして":["You're welcome","The response when someone thanks you. In everyday speech, people also often say 「いえいえ」 or 「とんでもない」.","\"Thanks for helping me.\" \"You're welcome.\"","kind words"],"こどもの日":["Children's Day","May 5, originally 「端午の節句」, the Boys' Festival. Families fly carp streamers, display samurai dolls, and eat 「柏餅」 (rice cakes wrapped in oak leaves) and 「ちまき」.","For Children's Day, we put up carp streamers on the balcony.","season"],"立夏":["Beginning of Summer (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around May 5. Summer officially begins on the calendar, and fresh green leaves are at their most beautiful.","Now that summer has begun, the sun is getting stronger little by little.","season"],"腐っても鯛":["Quality endures, even past its prime","Literally 'even a rotten sea bream is still a sea bream,' it means something truly fine keeps its value even after it's past its best. The sea bream is a prized, celebratory fish in Japan.","It's old, but quality is quality: a watch from this brand still sells for a good price.","proverb"],"一攫千金":["getting rich quick; striking it rich","Literally 'grabbing a thousand pieces of gold in one handful.' It means making a huge fortune all at once without much effort.","Dreaming of striking it rich, I bought a lottery ticket.","four-character idiom"],"手が離せない":["to be tied up; can't get away","Literally 'can't take one's hands off.' It means you're busy with something and can't break away to do anything else.","I'm in the middle of cooking and can't get away right now, so I'll call you later.","idiom"],"おやすみなさい":["Good night","Said before going to bed or when parting at night. Between friends and family, the shorter 「おやすみ」 is common.","I have to get up early again tomorrow, so I'm heading to bed. Good night.","kind words"],"人は見かけによらぬもの":["Don't judge a book by its cover","It means you can't judge a person's true character or ability by appearance alone. The English equivalent is 'Don't judge a book by its cover.'","That senpai who looks so scary is actually really kind. You can't judge people by their looks.","proverb"],"五月晴れ":["clear May weather","Today it usually means a fresh, sunny day in May. Originally it referred to a rare sunny day in the fifth month of the old lunar calendar, which falls in the rainy season.","Under the clear May sky, our family went out for a picnic.","season"],"七転八起":["Fall seven times, get up eight","Literally 'falling down seven times and getting up eight,' it means never losing heart no matter how often you fail. Be careful: it means something completely different from 「七転八倒」 (writhing in agony).","In the spirit of 'fall seven times, get up eight,' I'm going to try again.","four-character idiom"],"足が出る":["to go over budget","Literally 'one's feet stick out,' it means spending more than you had planned. It's often used as 「予算から足が出る」.","I spent too much on the trip and went over budget.","idiom"],"行ってらっしゃい":["See you later; have a good day","What you say when seeing a family member off; the person leaving says 「行ってきます」. It's a warm exchange heard in Japanese homes every day.","\"I'm off!\" \"See you later. Be careful!\"","kind words"],"郷に入っては郷に従え":["When in Rome, do as the Romans do","It means that when you go somewhere new, you should follow the local customs. The English equivalent is 'When in Rome, do as the Romans do.'","When in Rome, do as the Romans do, so in Japan I'm careful to sort my trash properly.","proverb"],"新緑":["fresh green leaves","The bright, tender new leaves that trees put out in early summer. The word evokes a fresh, vivid green.","In the season of fresh greenery, just walking in the mountains gives me energy.","season"],"一汁一菜":["one soup, one side dish; a simple meal","A simple meal of rice with just one bowl of soup and one side dish. It reflects a traditional Japanese approach to eating.","On busy days, one soup and one side dish is plenty.","four-character idiom"],"首をかしげる":["to tilt one's head in puzzlement","Describes tilting your head to one side because you don't understand something or have doubts. English might say 'to scratch one's head' for the same feeling.","His explanation left everyone scratching their heads.","idiom"],"焦らなくていいよ":["There's no need to rush","Comforts someone who feels rushed or anxious, telling them it's fine to take their time. 「焦る」 means to be impatient or flustered.","You don't have to give me an answer right away. There's no rush.","kind words"],"笑う門には福来る":["Fortune comes to those who smile","It means good fortune naturally comes to a home that is always full of laughter. Literally 'luck comes to the gate where people laugh.'","Don't be down. Good luck comes to those who smile!","proverb"],"風薫る":["fragrant breeze of early summer","Describes the fresh early-summer breeze blowing through new green leaves, as if it carried a fragrance. Letters often open with 「風薫る季節となりました」 (the season of fragrant breezes has arrived).","How have you been in this May of fragrant breezes?","season"],"本末転倒":["putting the cart before the horse","Means getting what's important and what's secondary backward. The English equivalent is 'putting the cart before the horse.'","If you get hurt exercising for your health, that defeats the whole purpose.","four-character idiom"],"口が堅い":["tight-lipped; good at keeping secrets","Literally 'one's mouth is hard,' it describes someone who can keep a secret. It's the opposite of 「口が軽い」.","He can keep a secret, so it's safe to confide in him.","idiom"],"いつでも話聞くよ":["I'm always here to listen","Tells someone who's struggling that they can come and talk to you any time. In casual speech, the particle 「を」 after 「話」 is dropped.","Don't keep it all bottled up. I'm always here to listen.","kind words"],"好きこそものの上手なれ":["You get good at what you love","It means that when you love something, you throw yourself into it and naturally get better and better. In other words, passion is the best teacher.","Your Japanese got this good because you love anime? That proves you get good at what you love.","proverb"],"木漏れ日":["sunlight filtering through the leaves","Sunlight streaming down through the gaps between leaves on a tree. It's often cited as a beautiful Japanese word that's hard to translate directly into other languages.","Walking through the sunlight filtering down through the trees calms my mind.","season"],"自業自得":["You reap what you sow; it serves you right","From Buddhism: you yourself bear the consequences of your own actions. In Japanese it's used almost exclusively for bad outcomes.","You stayed up all night gaming and overslept, so you've only got yourself to blame.","four-character idiom"],"鼻にかける":["to brag about; to be stuck-up about","Means showing off your strengths or achievements. It's used as 「〜を鼻にかける」, literally 'to hang something on one's nose.'","She's stuck-up about her good grades.","idiom"],"上を向いて歩こう":["Let's look up as we walk","A famous song by Kyu Sakamoto (lyrics by Rokusuke Ei); the lyrics continue 「涙がこぼれないように」 (so the tears won't fall). Overseas, it became a huge hit under the title 'Sukiyaki.'","When things are hard is exactly when you should hold your head up and keep walking.","kind words"],"木を見て森を見ず":["can't see the forest for the trees","It means focusing so much on details that you miss the big picture. It's exactly like English 'can't see the forest for the trees.'","Memorizing only vocabulary without ever reading texts is seeing the trees but not the forest.","proverb"],"衣替え":["seasonal change of clothes","Changing your wardrobe with the seasons. Traditionally, Japanese schools and companies switch to summer clothes on June 1 and to winter clothes on October 1.","Today's the seasonal changeover, so our uniforms are short-sleeved now.","season"],"八方美人":["someone who tries to please everyone","Literally 'a beauty from all eight directions,' but in Japanese it's mostly negative, describing someone who tries to please everyone and has no principles. Despite 「美人」 (beautiful person), it has nothing to do with looks.","If you try to please everyone, in the end nobody will really trust you.","four-character idiom"],"腹が立つ":["to get angry","Japanese uses 「腹」 (belly) to talk about emotions, so 「腹が立つ」, literally 'one's belly stands up,' means getting angry. It's a very common everyday expression.","They broke their promise, and I was really angry.","idiom"],"助かりました":["That was a big help","Says that someone's help got you out of a tight spot. It's more specific than just 「ありがとう」 and is very common at work.","Thank you for putting the materials together. It was a huge help.","kind words"],"雨降って地固まる":["After the rain, the ground grows firm","Literally 'after it rains, the ground hardens,' it means relationships or situations often become stronger after a conflict or hardship. Compare English 'Every cloud has a silver lining.'","We had a huge fight, but the ground is firmer after the rain: we get along better now than before.","proverb"],"芒種":["Grain in Ear (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around June 6. It's the time for planting grains with awns, such as rice and wheat.","Around Grain in Ear, rice planting is in full swing in the paddies.","season"],"試行錯誤":["trial and error","A translation of the English 'trial and error,' it means trying things over and over and improving through failure. English speakers will find it easy to remember.","After a lot of trial and error, I finally perfected my new recipe.","four-character idiom"],"梅雨入り":["start of the rainy season","The beginning of the rainy season, officially announced by the Japan Meteorological Agency. In the Kanto region it usually starts in early June and brings more than a month of cloudy, rainy weather.","The rainy season has started in Kanto too, and I can't go anywhere without an umbrella.","season"],"耳を傾ける":["to listen carefully; to lend an ear","Literally 'to tilt one's ear,' it means listening attentively to what others say or think. English says 'to lend an ear.'","A leader should listen to the opinions of their team.","idiom"],"応援してるよ":["I'm rooting for you","Shows that you're cheering someone on and are always on their side. It's used for exams, competitions, new challenges, and so on.","Your new job is going to go great. I'm rooting for you!","kind words"],"遠くの親類より近くの他人":["A good neighbor is better than a distant relative","It means that in an emergency, relatives who live far away are less help than the neighbors or friends close by. English has a similar proverb: 'Better a neighbor near than a brother far off.'","A neighbor nearby is worth more than a relative far away. Let's stay on good terms with the people next door.","proverb"],"紫陽花":["hydrangea","The signature flower of the rainy season. Its color changes depending on the acidity of the soil, and the hydrangeas of places like Kamakura are famous.","Hydrangeas look their most beautiful when they're wet with rain.","season"],"心機一転":["a fresh start; turning over a new leaf","Means completely changing your outlook because of some event, often when moving, changing jobs, or at the New Year. It's a very common expression in Japanese.","Let's use the move as a fresh start and really buckle down on studying.","four-character idiom"],"猫の手も借りたい":["so busy you'd take help from anyone","Literally 'I'd even like to borrow a cat's paws,' it describes being incredibly busy and short-handed. It's often used as 「猫の手も借りたいほど忙しい」.","The end of the year is so busy I'd take help from anyone, even the cat.","idiom"],"おかげさまで":["Thanks to you; I'm doing well, thank you","A modest reply when someone asks how you are, meaning things are going well thanks to everyone's support. You can use it even if the person didn't directly help you.","\"How are you?\" \"I'm doing well, thank you.\"","kind words"],"五月雨":["early summer rain (the rainy season)","Rain in the fifth month of the old lunar calendar, in other words the rainy season. The phrase 「五月雨式に」 also describes things that happen in scattered, on-and-off bits.","The early summer rain keeps falling, and the laundry just won't dry.","season"],"猫に小判":["pearls before swine","Literally 'gold coins to a cat,' it means giving something valuable to someone who can't appreciate it is pointless. The English equivalent is 'casting pearls before swine.'","An expensive kitchen knife for me, when I never cook? That's pearls before swine.","proverb"],"才色兼備":["both brains and beauty","Describes a woman who has both talent and good looks. It's generally used only for women.","She has both brains and beauty, and everyone looks up to her.","four-character idiom"],"猫をかぶる":["to put on an innocent act","Literally 'to wear a cat,' it means hiding your true nature and acting meek and well-behaved in front of others. English speakers might say someone is 'on their best behavior.'","He acts all quiet and polite when you first meet him, but he's actually really loud.","idiom"],"また明日":["See you tomorrow","An everyday phrase for parting. It carries a reassuring sense that you'll see each other again tomorrow.","Today was so much fun. See you tomorrow!","kind words"],"夏至":["Summer Solstice (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around June 21, the day with the most daylight of the year. In Japan it falls during the rainy season, so the sun often isn't even visible.","On the summer solstice, it's still light out even after seven in the evening.","season"],"沈黙は金、雄弁は銀":["Silence is golden","From the Western proverb 'Speech is silver, silence is golden.' It means that being eloquent is good, but knowing when to keep quiet is even more valuable.","You were right not to say anything unnecessary at the meeting. Silence is golden, speech is silver.","proverb"],"無病息災":["good health and safety","Means staying healthy and free from misfortune. It's often used when praying at shrines.","I prayed at the shrine for my family's good health.","four-character idiom"],"手を抜く":["to cut corners; to slack off","Literally 'to pull out one's hand,' it means not putting proper effort into work you're supposed to do. English says 'to cut corners.'","If you cut corners on the cleaning, it'll be obvious right away.","idiom"],"梅雨の晴れ間":["a break in the rainy season","A brief sunny spell during the rainy season. People make the most of it to air out their futons and do the laundry.","Let's air out the futons while there's a break in the rain.","season"],"一歩ずつでいい":["One step at a time is fine","Comforts someone in a hurry, telling them they don't have to be perfect all at once and can move forward slowly. It's like English 'Take it one step at a time.'","Don't rush. One step at a time is all it takes.","kind words"],"孝行したい時に親はなし":["By the time you want to repay your parents, they're gone","It means that by the time you want to show your parents gratitude, they may no longer be alive. It urges people to be good to their parents while they still can.","They say that by the time you want to repay your parents, they're gone, so I should visit home once in a while.","proverb"],"付和雷同":["blindly following others","Describes someone with no opinion of their own who just goes along with whatever others say. English calls this 'following the herd.'","Don't just follow the crowd. Have opinions of your own.","four-character idiom"],"鯖を読む":["to fudge the numbers (often one's age)","Mackerel spoils quickly, so fishmongers are said to have counted them in a hurry and often got the numbers wrong. It came to mean deliberately misstating figures, especially shaving years off one's age.","She had been knocking a full five years off her age.","idiom"],"君ならできる":["You can do it","Expresses confidence in someone's ability. 「なら」 means 'if it's you,' so the feeling is 'you of all people can do it.'","No need to worry. If anyone can do it, you can.","kind words"],"山開き":["the opening of the climbing season","The day the climbing season officially opens and a mountain may be climbed. Mt. Fuji traditionally opens on July 1 (on the Yamanashi side).","My friends and I are waiting for Mt. Fuji to open for the season, and we've made plans to climb it.","season"],"隣の芝生は青い":["The grass is always greener on the other side","From the English proverb, it means other people's things always look better than your own. Literally 'the neighbor's lawn is green.'","They say the grass is always greener on the other side, but my own company isn't so bad after all.","proverb"],"波瀾万丈":["full of ups and downs; eventful","Describes a life or story full of dramatic twists and turns. It's often used as 「波瀾万丈の人生」 (an eventful life).","My grandfather lived a life full of ups and downs.","four-character idiom"],"胸がいっぱいになる":["to be overwhelmed with emotion","Describes feelings such as being moved, joy, or sadness filling your chest so much that you can't speak. English similarly says 'my heart was full.'","At the graduation ceremony, my heart was full.","idiom"],"心配してくれてありがとう":["Thanks for worrying about me","Thanks someone for their concern. 「てくれて」 shows that the other person did something for your benefit.","Thanks for worrying about me. I'm completely better now.","kind words"],"捨てる神あれば拾う神あり":["When one door closes, another opens","Literally 'if there's a god who throws you away, there's also a god who picks you up,' it means that if someone rejects you, someone else will help you, so don't despair. The English equivalent is 'When one door closes, another opens.'","So one company turned you down. When one door closes, another one opens.","proverb"],"七夕":["Tanabata, the Star Festival","In Japan, Tanabata is usually celebrated on July 7 of the modern calendar. People write their wishes on colorful paper strips called 「短冊」 and hang them on bamboo branches.","On my Tanabata wish strip, I wrote, 'I hope my Japanese gets better.'","season"],"開口一番":["the first thing out of one's mouth","Refers to the very first words someone says as soon as they start speaking. It's often used as 「開口一番〜と言った」.","The first thing the department head said was, 'I have some good news today.'","four-character idiom"],"胸を打つ":["to touch one's heart","Literally 'to strike the chest,' it means to move people deeply. English says something 'touches your heart.'","His speech touched the hearts of many people.","idiom"],"よかったね":["That's great! I'm so happy for you","Said when you're genuinely glad about someone's good news. It shows that you share in their joy.","\"I got into my first-choice school!\" \"That's wonderful!\"","kind words"],"亀の甲より年の功":["With age comes wisdom","A pun: 甲 (a turtle's shell) and 功 (merit) are both read こう. It means the experience of older people is very valuable, like English 'older and wiser.'","My grandmother's wisdom really helped me out. With age comes wisdom.","proverb"],"疑心暗鬼":["paranoia; seeing enemies everywhere","It comes from the saying 'a suspicious mind conjures up demons in the dark.' Once you start doubting, everything begins to look suspicious.","Ever since I was lied to once, I've been suspicious of everything.","four-character idiom"],"尻に敷く":["to have one's husband under one's thumb","Literally 'to sit on someone,' it means the wife wears the pants in the family. A husband who gets bossed around is said to be 「尻に敷かれる」, like English 'henpecked.'","He's completely under his wife's thumb.","idiom"],"風鈴":["wind chime","A small bell hung from the eaves, whose clear ringing makes people feel cooler. It's one of the classic symbols of summer in Japan.","Hearing the wind chime makes me feel a little cooler.","season"],"雨ニモマケズ":["Unbeaten by the rain","The opening of a poem by Kenji Miyazawa, followed by 「風ニモマケズ」 (unbeaten by the wind). The original is written in katakana and describes an ideal person who is strong, simple, and thinks of others.","Unbeaten by rain, unbeaten by wind, let's keep at it steadily every day.","kind words"],"弘法も筆の誤り":["Even experts make mistakes","Kobo Daishi (Kukai) was a famous calligrapher, yet even he sometimes made mistakes with his brush. It means even masters slip up; English says 'Even Homer nods.'","Even professional interpreters sometimes mishear things. Even the experts make mistakes.","proverb"],"言語道断":["outrageous; inexcusable","Originally a Buddhist term meaning that profound truth cannot be expressed in words. In Japanese it shifted to mean 'so bad there are no words for it.'","Drunk driving is absolutely inexcusable.","four-character idiom"],"肩を持つ":["to take someone's side","Literally 'to hold someone's shoulder,' it means backing one side in an argument. It often implies favoritism.","My mom always takes my little sister's side.","idiom"],"梅雨明け":["end of the rainy season","The end of the rainy season, officially announced by the Japan Meteorological Agency. In the Kanto region it usually comes in mid-to-late July, and then real midsummer begins.","The moment the rainy season ended, we started getting one scorching day after another.","season"],"無理しないでね":["Don't push yourself too hard","Tells someone you care about not to overdo it or force themselves; it's common between friends and family. To someone older or higher in status, say 「ご無理なさらないでください」.","I know you're busy, but don't push yourself too hard, OK?","kind words"],"暑中お見舞い申し上げます":["Wishing you well in the summer heat","The opening line of 「暑中見舞い」, the greeting cards sent to friends and relatives in midsummer. They're usually sent between the end of the rainy season and 立秋.","Wishing you well in the summer heat. The hot days continue, so please take care of yourself.","season"],"情けは人のためならず":["Kindness comes back to you","It means that being kind to others eventually comes back to benefit you. It's often misunderstood as 'being kind to people only spoils them,' but that's incorrect.","Let's help people who are in trouble. Kindness always comes back around.","proverb"],"大暑":["Greater Heat (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, falling around July 23. It marks the hottest time of the year.","Today is Greater Heat, and just stepping outside makes me sweat buckets.","season"],"一生懸命":["with all one's might; as hard as one can","Originally 「一所懸命」, describing samurai risking their lives to defend their one plot of land; it later came to mean 'trying as hard as you can.' It's one of the most commonly used words in Japanese.","He studies Japanese as hard as he can every day.","four-character idiom"],"舌を巻く":["to be amazed; to be deeply impressed","Literally 'to curl one's tongue,' it describes being astonished by and full of admiration for someone's ability. It's close to English 'to be blown away.'","I was blown away by how good his Japanese is.","idiom"],"会えてうれしい":["I'm so happy to see you","Expresses joy at meeting a friend or seeing someone after a long time. It sounds more heartfelt than a polite formula.","It's been so long! I'm so happy to see you.","kind words"],"親の心子知らず":["Children never understand their parents' love","It means children don't realize how much their parents care and worry about them. Literally 'the child doesn't know the parent's heart.'","Calling your mom annoying for phoning every day? Children never understand how much their parents care.","proverb"],"花火大会":["fireworks festival","Large fireworks displays held all over Japan in summer. Many people go in yukata, and Tokyo's Sumida River Fireworks Festival is especially famous.","I put on a yukata and went to the fireworks festival with my friends.","season"],"絶体絶命":["a desperate situation; with one's back to the wall","Describes being in a crisis with no way out. Note that it's written 「絶体」, not 「絶対」, a mistake even Japanese people often make.","Bottom of the ninth, two outs, and the team's back is against the wall.","four-character idiom"],"首を長くする":["to wait eagerly; to look forward to","Literally 'to make one's neck long,' it describes waiting eagerly for something. English has a similar image in 'to crane one's neck.'","The children are eagerly counting down to Christmas.","idiom"],"元気出して":["Cheer up!","Comforts someone who is down or disappointed and encourages them to pick themselves up. 「元気」 means energy or spirits, so it's literally 'bring out your energy.'","Don't get discouraged just because you lost once. Cheer up!","kind words"],"覆水盆に返らず":["What's done cannot be undone","Literally 'spilled water won't return to the tray.' From an ancient Chinese story about the statesman Jiang Ziya and his wife, it means that what's done cannot be undone; English says 'There's no use crying over spilled milk.'","Spilled water can't be put back, so be careful what you say.","proverb"],"竜頭蛇尾":["starting strong but fizzling out","Literally 'dragon's head, snake's tail,' it describes something that begins impressively but ends weakly. In English you'd say it 'started with a bang and ended with a whimper.'","That plan started with a bang and ended with a whimper.","four-character idiom"],"夏バテ":["summer fatigue","Exhaustion and loss of appetite caused by the summer heat. To fight it off, Japanese people traditionally eat eel on 「土用の丑の日」, a 'Day of the Ox' in midsummer.","The heat has killed my appetite, but I have to eat properly.","season"],"腹が黒い":["scheming; black-hearted","Literally 'one's belly is black,' it describes someone who seems nice on the surface but is scheming inside. English similarly says 'black-hearted.'","He looks kind, but he's actually quite a schemer.","idiom"],"ただいま":["I'm home","What you say when you get home; your family answers 「おかえり」. It's one of the warmest phrases in everyday Japanese life.","I'm home. Ah, there really is no place like home.","kind words"],"雨垂れ石を穿つ":["Constant dripping wears away the stone","Raindrops falling from the eaves can bore through stone over many years, meaning that persistence pays off in the end. English has exactly the same proverb: 'Constant dripping wears away the stone.'","Constant dripping wears away the stone: a little effort every day adds up to great strength.","proverb"],"立秋":["Beginning of Autumn (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around August 7. Autumn begins on the calendar, but in reality the heat is still intense.","It may be the beginning of autumn, but the heat is still brutal.","season"],"残暑お見舞い申し上げます":["Wishing you well in the lingering heat","A summer greeting used after 立秋: before it you use 「暑中お見舞い」, and after it you switch to 「残暑お見舞い」. These cards are usually sent until the end of August.","Wishing you well in the lingering summer heat. The fierce heat continues, so please take good care of yourself.","season"],"一石二鳥":["killing two birds with one stone","Literally 'one stone, two birds,' it comes from the English 'kill two birds with one stone.' So English speakers will find it easy to remember.","Biking to work is exercise and saves money too: two birds with one stone.","four-character idiom"],"犬猿の仲":["to fight like cats and dogs","Literally 'a dog-and-monkey relationship'; in Japan, dogs and monkeys are said to be natural enemies. English uses cats and dogs for the same idea.","Those two fight like cats and dogs, so it's better not to put them on the same team.","idiom"],"笑顔が一番":["A smile is what matters most","Encourages people to keep smiling, because a smile beats everything else. 「一番」 means 'number one' or 'the best.'","A lot has happened, but in the end, a smile is what matters most.","kind words"],"帰省":["going back to one's hometown","Returning to your hometown to visit family when you live somewhere else. Obon and New Year's are the two big times for this in Japan, and the resulting travel rush is called 「帰省ラッシュ」.","During the Obon holidays, I went back to my parents' home for the first time in three years.","season"],"お盆":["Obon (festival of the ancestors)","A festival for welcoming the spirits of ancestors back home, usually held from August 13 to 16. Many companies give 「お盆休み」 (Obon holidays), and people return to their hometowns to visit graves and join in Bon dancing.","At Obon, the family gathers to welcome our ancestors home.","season"],"猿も木から落ちる":["Even monkeys fall from trees","It means that even the most skilled people sometimes fail. English speakers would say 'Nobody's perfect.'","The teacher wrote a kanji wrong. Even monkeys fall from trees!","proverb"],"良妻賢母":["a good wife and wise mother","Literally 'good wife, wise mother' (良 means good and 賢 means wise). From the late nineteenth century onward, it served as a slogan for women's education in Japan.","My grandmother was known as a good wife and a wise mother.","four-character idiom"],"送り火":["send-off fires for ancestral spirits","Fires lit on the last day of Obon to send the ancestors' spirits back. The most famous is Kyoto's 「五山送り火」 on August 16.","Watching the Gozan send-off fires in Kyoto, I felt that summer was coming to an end.","season"],"道草を食う":["to dawdle on the way","Literally 'to eat the grass by the road,' like a horse stopping to graze along the way. It means wasting time on other things when you're supposed to be going somewhere.","I dawdled on my way home from school and got scolded by my mom.","idiom"],"いつでも味方だよ":["I'm always on your side","「味方」 means an ally, someone on your side. The phrase says you'll support the other person no matter what happens.","Whatever happens, I'll always be on your side.","kind words"],"旅は道連れ世は情け":["On a journey, companions; in life, kindness","Literally 'on a journey, a companion; in this world, compassion.' It means a traveling companion gives you peace of mind on the road, and getting through life takes kindness and helping one another.","Journeys need companions and life needs kindness: some people I met on my trip helped me out.","proverb"],"臨機応変":["adapting to the situation","Means handling things flexibly according to the circumstances. English might say 'to play it by ear.'","Please don't just go by the manual; deal with things flexibly as they come up.","four-character idiom"],"足を引っ張る":["to hold someone back; to drag someone down","Literally 'to pull someone's leg,' but it has nothing to do with teasing! It means hindering others or being a burden on the team, like English 'to drag someone down.'","I'll do my best not to drag the team down.","idiom"],"ゆっくり休んでね":["Get some good rest","Caring words for someone who is tired, sick, or has just finished a busy stretch. 「ゆっくり」 means slowly, without hurrying.","Go to bed early tonight and get some good rest.","kind words"],"処暑":["End of Heat (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around August 23. 「処」 here means 'to stop,' as the summer heat gradually eases.","Now that the End of Heat has passed, the mornings and evenings have gotten a little cooler.","season"],"歳月人を待たず":["Time waits for no one","From a line by the Chinese poet Tao Yuanming, 'Strive while you can; the years wait for no one,' it means time never comes back, so make the most of it. English says 'Time and tide wait for no man.'","Time waits for no one. Let's do what we want to do now.","proverb"],"有言実行":["walking the talk; doing what you say","A Japanese coinage modeled on 「不言実行」, it means announcing a goal publicly and then achieving it. The English equivalent is 'to walk the talk.'","He's a man of his word: he won the tournament, just as he said he would.","four-character idiom"],"気が合う":["to get along; to click","Means having similar personalities or interests and getting along well. In English, two people 'click.'","She and I clicked from the moment we first met.","idiom"],"よく頑張ったね":["You did a great job","Recognizes the effort someone has made, whatever the result. It's very warm praise.","Whatever the result, you did a great job.","kind words"],"負けるが勝ち":["Losing is winning","It means that giving in or admitting defeat for now can work to your advantage in the long run. English has a related idea in 'Lose the battle, win the war.'","In fights between brothers, the one who gives in wins. It's better to apologize first.","proverb"],"正真正銘":["the real deal; genuine","Means absolutely nothing fake about it: the genuine article. It's often used to stress that something is authentic.","This is a genuine Picasso painting.","four-character idiom"],"骨が折れる":["to be hard work; to take a lot of effort","Literally 'bones break,' but as an idiom it only means that something takes a lot of effort, not that anyone actually breaks a bone. English has a similar image in 'back-breaking work.'","Putting these materials together is hard work.","idiom"],"ごちそうさまでした":["Thank you for the meal","Words of thanks said after eating. 「馳走」 originally meant running around to prepare a meal, so the phrase thanks the other person for all the trouble they went to.","Thank you for the meal. It was delicious.","kind words"],"防災の日":["Disaster Prevention Day","Established in memory of the Great Kanto Earthquake of September 1, 1923. On this day, disaster drills are held across Japan and people check their emergency supplies.","Since it's Disaster Prevention Day, I checked our emergency grab bag at home.","season"],"読書百遍義自ずから見る":["Read a book a hundred times and its meaning becomes clear","It comes from the commentary on the Chinese history Records of the Three Kingdoms. It means that if you read a difficult book over and over, you will naturally come to understand it.","Even a difficult text will make sense if you read it again and again: read it a hundred times and the meaning reveals itself.","proverb"],"晴耕雨読":["farming on sunny days, reading on rainy days","Literally 'plow when it's sunny, read when it rains,' it describes a leisurely country life. It's often used to describe an ideal retirement.","After I retire, I want to live in the countryside, farming when it's sunny and reading when it rains.","four-character idiom"],"腹を割る":["to open up; to speak frankly","Literally 'to split open one's belly,' it means saying what you really think without hiding anything. English might say 'to have a heart-to-heart.'","Let's sit down and have a heart-to-heart talk.","idiom"],"気にしないで":["Don't worry about it","Reassures someone who has apologized or feels bad, telling them not to let it bother them. 「気にする」 means to mind or worry about something.","Being a little late is nothing. Don't worry about it.","kind words"],"立つ鳥跡を濁さず":["Leave things as you found them","Literally 'a bird that flies off doesn't muddy the water behind it.' It means that when you leave a place, you should tidy up and settle everything properly.","Like a bird that leaves the water clear, I cleaned my room thoroughly before moving out.","proverb"],"真剣勝負":["a serious contest; playing for keeps","Originally a duel fought with real swords instead of wooden ones. It now means an all-out contest where both sides are completely serious.","Even if it's just a practice match, I intend to go all out.","four-character idiom"],"白露":["White Dew (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around September 8, when white dew begins to form on grass and leaves. Autumn is clearly in the air.","Around White Dew, dewdrops glisten on the grass in the morning.","season"],"重陽の節句":["Chrysanthemum Festival (the Double Ninth)","September 9, one of the five seasonal festivals, also called 「菊の節句」 (the Chrysanthemum Festival). Customs include admiring chrysanthemums and drinking chrysanthemum sake to pray for long life.","For the Chrysanthemum Festival, we display chrysanthemums and pray for long life.","season"],"目に余る":["too much to overlook; intolerable","Describes words or actions so excessive that you can't just stand by and watch. Literally 'too much for the eyes.'","His attitude lately is more than I can put up with.","idiom"],"待ってるね":["I'll be waiting","Shows that you're looking forward to someone coming or coming back. It has a friendly, affectionate tone.","I'll be waiting in front of the station. Take your time.","kind words"],"仏の顔も三度まで":["Even a saint's patience has limits","Literally 'even the Buddha's face (can be stroked) only three times,' it means that even the gentlest person will get angry if wronged again and again. English might say 'Three strikes and you're out.'","This is the third time you've been late. Even my patience has limits, you know.","proverb"],"四苦八苦":["struggling desperately","Originally a Buddhist term for all the sufferings of life, such as birth, aging, sickness, and death. In everyday Japanese it means having a really hard time with something.","I'm having a terrible time with honorific language, since I'm not used to it.","four-character idiom"],"鼻が高い":["to be proud","Literally 'one's nose is high.' It means feeling proud because of your own or a family member's achievements, and unlike English 'nose in the air,' it isn't negative.","My son won an award, and as his parent I'm very proud.","idiom"],"秋の夜長":["the long autumn nights","A seasonal phrase for the lengthening nights of autumn. It suggests a time well suited to reading, listening to music, and quiet evenings.","I love reading novels at a leisurely pace on long autumn nights.","season"],"大丈夫、なんとかなる":["It's okay, it'll work out somehow","「なんとかなる」 means 'somehow it'll work out' or 'we'll manage.' It's often used to cheer up yourself or others with an optimistic tone.","Even if you fail, it's okay. Things will work out somehow.","kind words"],"親しき仲にも礼儀あり":["Good manners even among close friends","It means that no matter how close you are, you should still keep proper courtesy and respect boundaries. Literally 'even in close relationships there is etiquette.'","Good manners matter even between close friends. Return the things you borrow.","proverb"],"唯我独尊":["self-centered arrogance","From words the Buddha is said to have spoken at birth: 'In heaven and on earth, I alone am honored.' In everyday Japanese it's mostly negative, describing someone conceited and arrogant.","Everyone is fed up with his arrogant, me-first attitude.","four-character idiom"],"水に流す":["to let bygones be bygones","Literally 'to wash it away in water,' it means forgetting past grudges and conflicts completely. The English equivalent is 'Let bygones be bygones.'","Let's let bygones be bygones and make up.","idiom"],"彼岸花":["red spider lily","A red flower that blooms around the autumn equinox, named after the autumn 「彼岸」 period when it appears. It's also called 「曼珠沙華」.","Bright red spider lilies were blooming along the paths between the rice paddies.","season"],"気持ちだけでうれしい":["The thought alone makes me happy","Said when someone offers to help or to give you something, meaning you appreciate the thought and they don't need to go to any trouble. English says 'It's the thought that counts.'","Just the thought makes me happy. Thank you so much.","kind words"],"百里を行く者は九十を半ばとす":["Ninety miles is only halfway on a hundred-mile journey","From the ancient Chinese text Strategies of the Warring States, it means that the closer you are to finishing, the more careful you must be, because the last stretch is the hardest. Don't let up.","My graduation thesis is almost done, but ninety miles is only halfway on a hundred-mile road.","proverb"],"秋分":["Autumn Equinox (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around September 23, when day and night are nearly equal. 「秋分の日」 is a national holiday in Japan and a day for visiting family graves.","On Autumn Equinox Day, I went to visit my grandparents' grave.","season"],"門外不出":["closely guarded; never shown to outsiders","Describes a precious object or skill that is never taken out of the house or revealed to outsiders. It's often used for secret recipes and family treasures.","This restaurant's sauce is made from a closely guarded secret recipe.","four-character idiom"],"十五夜":["the night of the harvest moon","The moonlit night of the 15th day of the eighth lunar month. People view the moon with offerings of dumplings and pampas grass, which is called 「お月見」; the date changes every year but usually falls in mid-to-late September.","On the night of the harvest moon, we ate moon-viewing dumplings and gazed at the beautiful full moon.","season"],"足が棒になる":["legs as stiff as sticks","Literally 'one's legs turn into sticks.' It describes legs that are stiff and aching from walking or standing too long.","After walking around all day, my legs were stiff as boards.","idiom"],"みんなちがって、みんないい":["We're all different, and we're all good","The final line of 「わたしと小鳥とすずと」 (Me, a Little Bird, and a Bell), a poem by the children's poet Misuzu Kaneko. It celebrates what makes each person unique.","Everyone's good at different things. We're all different, and we're all good.","kind words"],"玉磨かざれば光なし":["An unpolished gem won't shine","It means that even the most talented person can't shine without study and training. It's close to a line in the Chinese classic Book of Rites: 'Jade that is not carved will not become a vessel.'","Even if you have talent, an unpolished gem won't shine. Let's practice every day.","proverb"],"二束三文":["dirt cheap","Literally 'two bundles for three mon (old coins),' it describes something sold for next to nothing. It's a very Japanese expression.","I sold a pile of old books all together, but they only fetched a pittance.","four-character idiom"],"お茶を濁す":["to gloss over; to fudge","Literally 'to muddy the tea'; it's said that people who didn't know the tea ceremony would just stir the tea until it was cloudy to fake it. It means giving a vague answer to get by.","I couldn't answer the question, so I said something vague to gloss it over.","idiom"],"鰯雲":["mackerel sky; sardine clouds","Small patches of high cloud packed together like a school of sardines. It's a seasonal word (季語) for autumn; English calls this a 'mackerel sky.'","When I looked up, a mackerel sky was spreading across the heavens.","season"],"ありがとうを言いたくて":["I just wanted to say thank you","Here 「ありがとう」 is used as a noun, meaning you've reached out specifically to say thanks. It often opens a message, letter, or phone call, and sounds sincere and warm.","Sorry for calling out of the blue. I just wanted to say thank you.","kind words"],"時は金なり":["Time is money","It comes from the English proverb 'Time is money.' The idea is that time is as precious as money and shouldn't be wasted.","Time is money. Stop playing games all the time and go study.","proverb"],"一心不乱":["wholeheartedly; with undivided attention","Means doing something with complete focus, without letting anything distract you. Literally 'one heart, not disturbed.'","The children are completely absorbed in their drawings.","four-character idiom"],"食欲の秋":["autumn, the season of appetite","Autumn is harvest season, with plenty of delicious food such as new rice, Pacific saury, chestnuts, and matsutake mushrooms. That's why it's called 'the autumn of appetite.'","It's the season of good food, and I keep eating too much.","season"],"口に合う":["to suit one's taste","Means food agrees with your palate. When serving guests, people often say 「お口に合うかどうか」 (I'm not sure if it will suit your taste).","I'm not sure whether it will suit your taste, but please help yourself.","idiom"],"さすが":["As expected of you! Impressive!","Praises someone for living up to their reputation and being just as good as you expected. It's often used as 「さすがですね」 or 「さすが田中さん」.","You're done already? I'd expect nothing less from you.","kind words"],"寒露":["Cold Dew (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around October 8. The dew grows colder, and autumn is well advanced.","Since Cold Dew, the mornings and evenings have gotten really chilly.","season"],"石橋を叩いて渡る":["to look before you leap; to be extremely cautious","Literally 'to tap a stone bridge before crossing it' to make sure it's solid, it describes someone who is very careful. English says 'Look before you leap.'","He's the type who taps a stone bridge before crossing it, so he read the contract three times.","proverb"],"他力本願":["relying on others","Originally a term from Pure Land Buddhism, meaning rebirth in paradise through the power of Amida Buddha's vow. In modern Japanese it has taken on a negative meaning: depending on others for everything.","If you keep counting on other people, you'll never grow.","four-character idiom"],"手が届く":["within reach; affordable; nearing an age","Literally 'one's hand reaches,' it can mean a price is affordable. It can also describe approaching an age, as in 「四十に手が届く」 (to be pushing forty).","At this price, even I can afford it.","idiom"],"読書の秋":["autumn, the season for reading","Autumn's cool weather and long nights make it the perfect time for reading. It's mentioned together with 「食欲の秋」 and 「スポーツの秋」.","It's the season for reading, so I'll read a little every night.","season"],"泣いてもいいんだよ":["It's okay to cry","Tells someone who's hurting that they don't have to hold it in and can let their feelings out. It's a very gentle way to comfort someone.","When things are hard, it's okay to cry.","kind words"],"かわいい子には旅をさせよ":["If you love your child, let them see the world","It means that if you truly love your children, you should let them go out into the world and learn from hardship. Literally 'send the child you love on a journey.'","They say that if you love your child you should let them travel, so I agreed to my son studying abroad.","proverb"],"傍若無人":["acting as if no one else were around; inconsiderate","Literally 'as if there were no one beside you.' In Japanese it's clearly negative, describing someone who ignores the people around them and does as they please.","Talking loudly on the train is so inconsiderate.","four-character idiom"],"目がない":["to have a weakness for; to be crazy about","Literally 'to have no eyes,' 「〜に目がない」 means loving something so much that you lose all judgment about it. It can also mean having no eye for something, that is, poor judgment.","My big sister has a real weakness for sweets.","idiom"],"気をつけてね":["Take care; be careful","A common phrase when seeing someone off or reminding them to stay safe. It's like English 'Take care' or 'Be safe.'","The streets are dark at night, so be careful.","kind words"],"秋晴れ":["clear autumn weather","Fine autumn weather, when the air is crisp and the sky is high and deep blue. It has a fresh, clean feel.","The sports day was held on a lovely, clear autumn day.","season"],"海老で鯛を釣る":["to throw a sprat to catch a mackerel","Literally 'to catch a sea bream with a shrimp,' it means getting a big return for a small investment. English has a near-identical proverb: 'Throw a sprat to catch a mackerel.'","I gave them a little souvenir and they treated me to dinner. Talk about catching a sea bream with a shrimp!","proverb"],"大義名分":["a just cause; a justification","Originally the moral duty and proper place a subject was expected to uphold. In modern Japanese it usually means a legitimate reason for doing something, or a convenient excuse.","I bought a new computer on the grounds that it was 'for studying.'","four-character idiom"],"耳が痛い":["to hit a nerve; to strike close to home","It doesn't mean your ears actually hurt; it means someone has pointed out your faults and it's painful to hear. It's similar to English 'The truth hurts.'","My teacher's criticism stings, but it's right.","idiom"],"生きてるだけで丸もうけ":["Just being alive is a win","The motto of comedian Sanma Akashiya, meaning that simply being alive is already pure profit and everything else is a bonus. His daughter's stage name, IMALU, also comes from this phrase.","I don't worry about failing. Just being alive is a win.","kind words"],"霜降":["Frost's Descent (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around October 23. Frost begins to form, and the leaves gradually turn red.","With Frost's Descent here, the leaves in the mountains have started to change color.","season"],"論より証拠":["The proof is in the pudding","It means that rather than arguing, you should show the evidence, because facts are the most convincing. English says 'The proof of the pudding is in the eating.'","Proof beats argument. Let me actually make it and show you.","proverb"],"以心伝心":["unspoken understanding","Originally a Zen term for passing on teachings directly from mind to mind, without words. In everyday Japanese it describes friends or partners who understand each other without saying anything.","We've been partners for years, so we understand each other without words.","four-character idiom"],"頭が上がらない":["to be forever in someone's debt","Literally 'can't raise one's head.' It means being unable to stand up to someone because you owe them a great deal or are in the wrong.","I'm forever indebted to the senpai who looked after me.","idiom"],"明日があるさ":["There's always tomorrow","The title of a song sung by Kyu Sakamoto (lyrics by Yukio Aoshima). It means it's fine if today didn't go well, because tomorrow brings another chance.","Today didn't go well, but there's always tomorrow.","kind words"],"急いては事を仕損じる":["Haste makes waste","It means that rushing things often leads to failure, so stay calm and composed. The English equivalent is 'Haste makes waste.'","Haste makes waste. Calm down and read the question.","proverb"],"抱腹絶倒":["to double over with laughter","Literally 'clutching one's belly and falling over.' It describes laughing extremely hard at something hilarious, like English 'to be in stitches.'","The audience was in stitches at his stand-up routine.","four-character idiom"],"茶々を入れる":["to butt in with jokes; to heckle","Means interrupting someone with jokes or teasing while they're talking. It's often heard as 「茶々を入れないで」.","I'm trying to talk about something serious, so stop butting in.","idiom"],"ハロウィン":["Halloween","A holiday imported from the West. In Japan it's mainly about dressing up in costumes, and Shibuya in Tokyo draws huge crowds of costumed people every year.","On Halloween, the kids had fun dressing up as ghosts.","season"],"木枯らし":["cold winter wind","The strong north wind that blows from late autumn into early winter, stripping the leaves from the trees. The Japan Meteorological Agency announces the year's first one as 「木枯らし1号」.","The cold wind blew, and the leaves on the street trees all fell at once.","season"],"お互い様":["We all help each other; it goes both ways","Said when someone thanks you or apologizes. It means 'everyone needs help sometimes, so don't mention it.'","\"Thanks for helping me.\" \"Oh, not at all. We all help each other.\"","kind words"],"文化の日":["Culture Day","November 3, a national holiday in Japan dedicated to 'loving freedom and peace and promoting culture.' The Order of Culture is awarded on this day.","On Culture Day, the art museum nearby was open for free.","season"],"七転び八起き":["Fall seven times, get up eight","Literally 'fall down seven times, get up eight,' it means never giving up and always bouncing back, no matter how many times you fail. English speakers often quote it directly as 'Fall seven times, stand up eight.'","Even if I fail the exam, I'll take it again in the spirit of 'fall seven times, get up eight.'","proverb"],"平身低頭":["bowing low in apology","Means bowing deeply with your head lowered, humbly apologizing or pleading. In Japanese it's mostly used in situations where someone is apologizing.","The employee who made the mistake bowed low and apologized.","four-character idiom"],"腰を抜かす":["to be floored; to be scared stiff","Literally 'one's hips give out,' so that you can't stand up. It describes being extremely surprised or frightened.","When I heard the price, I nearly fell over.","idiom"],"立冬":["Beginning of Winter (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around November 7. Winter begins on the calendar from this day.","Now that winter has begun, maybe it's about time to bring out the kotatsu.","season"],"お大事に":["Get well soon; take care of yourself","Caring words for someone who is sick or injured. Staff at hospitals and pharmacies also say this to patients.","You have a cold? Please take care of yourself.","kind words"],"鬼に金棒":["unbeatable; even stronger than before","Literally 'an iron club for an ogre (鬼),' a creature that is already strong. It means someone strong gets an extra boost and becomes even stronger.","He's already great at English; if he could speak Japanese too, he'd be unstoppable.","proverb"],"自画自賛":["blowing one's own horn","Originally writing words of praise on your own painting, it came to mean praising yourself. English says 'to blow one's own horn.'","I know I'm blowing my own horn, but today's dinner turned out great.","four-character idiom"],"口を出す":["to butt in; to meddle","Means giving your opinion on, or interfering in, something that's none of your business. Literally 'to put out one's mouth.'","If this doesn't concern you, please don't butt in.","idiom"],"紅葉狩り":["autumn leaf viewing","Going to the mountains or to gardens in autumn to admire the red and yellow leaves. 「狩り」 normally means 'hunting,' but here it means going out to seek and enjoy something.","This weekend I went to Kyoto to see the autumn leaves.","season"],"早起きは三文の徳":["The early bird catches the worm","「三文」 means a tiny sum of money, so the saying means getting up early always brings some benefit. The English equivalent is 'The early bird catches the worm.'","They say the early bird catches the worm, so I've decided to study vocabulary in the mornings.","proverb"],"朝令暮改":["constantly changing orders","Literally 'an order in the morning, changed by evening.' It describes policies or instructions that keep flip-flopping.","The company's policy keeps flip-flopping, and things on the front lines are in chaos.","four-character idiom"],"七五三":["Shichi-Go-San (Seven-Five-Three Festival)","November 15, a festival to pray for the healthy growth of boys aged 3 and 5 and girls aged 3 and 7. Children visit shrines in kimono and get 「千歳飴」 (long, thin 'thousand-year candy').","For Shichi-Go-San, I took my daughter, dressed in a kimono, to visit the shrine.","season"],"揚げ足を取る":["to nitpick; to jump on someone's slip","Originally a term from sumo and similar sports for grabbing an opponent's raised leg to throw them. It came to mean seizing on someone's slip of the tongue or small mistake and making a big deal of it.","If you're always nitpicking what people say, they'll start to dislike you.","idiom"],"転ばぬ先の杖":["Better safe than sorry","Literally 'a cane before you fall,' it means preparing in advance so you don't fail. English says 'Better safe than sorry' or 'An ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure.'","Better safe than sorry, so I took out travel insurance.","proverb"],"起死回生":["a dramatic comeback","Literally 'bringing the dying back to life.' In Japanese it's mostly used for a dramatic turnaround in sports or business, as in 「起死回生のホームラン」 (a game-saving home run).","In the ninth inning came a game-saving, come-from-behind home run.","four-character idiom"],"匙を投げる":["to give up on; to throw in the towel","Literally 'to throw away the medicine spoon,' it originally described a doctor giving up on a patient who couldn't be cured. Now it means giving up on something completely, like English 'to throw in the towel.'","Even the teacher has given up on that student.","idiom"],"小春日和":["Indian summer","A warm, sunny, spring-like day in late autumn or early winter (the tenth month of the old calendar); it does not mean spring weather. English has a similar term: 'Indian summer.'","It's a lovely Indian summer day, so warm you hardly need a coat.","season"],"三人寄れば文殊の知恵":["Two heads are better than one","Monju (Manjushri) is the bodhisattva of wisdom, and the saying means that even ordinary people can come up with great ideas when three of them put their heads together. English says 'Two heads are better than one.'","Don't agonize over it by yourself; let's think it through together. Three heads together are as wise as Monju, after all.","proverb"],"一部始終":["the whole story from start to finish","「一部」 originally meant one complete book, so the phrase means 'from beginning to end.' It refers to the entire course of events, not just one part.","I told the police everything about the accident, from start to finish.","four-character idiom"],"勤労感謝の日":["Labor Thanksgiving Day","November 23, a national holiday in Japan. It's a day for honoring work and thanking one another for everyone's hard work.","On Labor Thanksgiving Day, I told my dad, 'Thanks for always working so hard.'","season"],"手を焼く":["to have one's hands full with","Literally 'to burn one's hands,' it means having a hard time dealing with someone or something. English might call such a person 'a real handful.'","My mischievous child is a real handful.","idiom"],"塵も積もれば山となる":["Every little bit adds up","Literally 'even dust, piled up, becomes a mountain,' it means that tiny things add up to something big. English has 'Every little bit helps' and 'Many a little makes a mickle.'","Even if you learn just five words a day, it all adds up: that's more than 1,800 words in a year.","proverb"],"十人十色":["To each their own","Literally 'ten people, ten colors,' it means everyone has different tastes and ideas. Note that 「十色」 is read といろ; English equivalents are 'To each their own' and 'Different strokes for different folks.'","Everyone's tastes are different, so it's better not to force yours on others.","four-character idiom"],"肩の荷が下りる":["a weight off one's shoulders","Literally 'the load comes off one's shoulders,' it describes the relief when a responsibility or burden is lifted. English uses the same image: 'a weight off my shoulders.'","Now that my presentation is over, it's finally a weight off my shoulders.","idiom"],"栴檀は双葉より芳し":["Genius shows itself early","「栴檀」 here means sandalwood, which is fragrant even as a tiny sprout. It means that outstanding people stand out even as young children.","Writing poems at five years old? Sandalwood is fragrant from its very first leaves.","proverb"],"右往左往":["running around in confusion","Literally 'going right, going left,' it describes rushing about in a panic, not knowing what to do. English says 'running around like a headless chicken.'","When the power suddenly went out, the passengers at the station were running around in confusion.","four-character idiom"],"目が回る":["to be dizzy; to be swamped","Literally 'one's eyes spin.' It can mean feeling dizzy, but it's often used to describe being incredibly busy.","This week I was so busy my head was spinning.","idiom"],"師走":["Shiwasu (December)","An old name for the twelfth month of the lunar calendar, now also used for December. Folk etymology says that at the end of the year even teachers (師) are so busy they run (走) around.","Now that December is here, everything's getting busy.","season"],"鳶が鷹を生む":["an extraordinary child from ordinary parents","Literally 'a kite gives birth to a hawk': the kite is a common bird, while the hawk is prized. It means that ordinary parents have produced an exceptional child.","Our kid got into the University of Tokyo? A kite really did give birth to a hawk!","proverb"],"単刀直入":["getting straight to the point","Means not beating around the bush and cutting right to the main issue. Literally 'charging in directly with a single sword.'","To get straight to the point, I'm against this plan.","four-character idiom"],"目から鱗が落ちる":["the scales fall from one's eyes","From the Bible, literally 'scales fall from one's eyes,' it means suddenly seeing something clearly. English has the same expression from the same source.","When I heard the teacher's explanation, the scales fell from my eyes.","idiom"],"三度目の正直":["Third time's the charm","It means that after two failed attempts, the third will surely succeed, and it's used to encourage people to try again. The English equivalent is 'Third time's the charm.'","I've failed twice, but I hope the third time's the charm and I pass.","proverb"],"三日坊主":["someone who never sticks with anything","Literally 'a three-day monk,' it describes someone who lacks staying power and quits quickly. It's a very Japanese expression.","I started keeping a diary, but once again I quit after three days.","four-character idiom"],"眉をひそめる":["to frown; to knit one's brows","Means frowning because you're displeased with or worried about someone's words or actions. Literally 'to draw one's eyebrows together.'","His rude language made the people around him frown.","idiom"],"灯台下暗し":["It's darkest under the lamp","Literally 'it's dark at the foot of the lamp stand'; here 「灯台」 means an old-fashioned candle stand, not a lighthouse. It means we tend to overlook what's right under our noses.","The glasses I'd been looking for were on top of my head. It's always darkest right under the lamp.","proverb"],"前代未聞":["unprecedented; unheard of","Refers to something no one has ever heard of before. It's mostly used for shocking events.","A power outage in the middle of a game? That's unheard of.","four-character idiom"],"忘年会":["year-end party","A party at the end of the year where coworkers or friends eat and drink together, forgetting the year's troubles and getting ready for the new year. Literally 'forget-the-year gathering.'","Our company's year-end party is this Friday.","season"],"顔が広い":["well-connected; to know a lot of people","Literally 'one's face is wide.' It describes someone who knows a lot of people and has a wide network.","Tanaka knows everyone, so he'll introduce you to someone.","idiom"],"蒔かぬ種は生えぬ":["No pain, no gain","Literally 'seeds you don't sow won't sprout,' it means you won't get good results without making an effort. English says 'No pain, no gain' or 'You reap what you sow.'","Seeds you don't sow won't sprout. There's no way you'll pass without studying.","proverb"],"一期一会":["once in a lifetime; treasure every encounter","From the tea ceremony: every meeting may be the only one in your lifetime, so treat it with sincerity. English has no set phrase for this, so it's often rendered 'treasure every encounter.'","We welcome each guest as though it were a once-in-a-lifetime meeting.","four-character idiom"],"舌鼓を打つ":["to smack one's lips; to savor","Literally 'to beat a drum with one's tongue,' it describes relishing delicious food and smacking your lips. Note the reading 「したつづみ」.","We savored the dinner at the inn.","idiom"],"雀百まで踊り忘れず":["Old habits die hard","Literally 'a sparrow never forgets how to dance (hop), even at a hundred,' it means habits formed when young last into old age. English says 'Old habits die hard.'","My grandfather still does his radio exercises every morning. Old habits die hard.","proverb"],"習い性となる":["Habit becomes second nature","From the Chinese classic Book of Documents, it means that habits built up over a long time become like part of your nature. English says 'Habit is second nature.'","Habit becomes second nature: listening to the Japanese news every morning is now just part of my routine.","proverb"],"鉄は熱いうちに打て":["Strike while the iron is hot","From the Western proverb, it means you should act at the right moment, without delay. It can also mean that people should be trained while they're young.","Strike while the iron is hot: try using the grammar you've learned right away.","proverb"],"馬の耳に念仏":["like talking to a brick wall","Literally 'chanting Buddhist prayers into a horse's ear,' it means that no matter how much you advise someone, it doesn't sink in. English says 'like talking to a brick wall.'","I've warned him so many times and he still won't listen. It's like talking to a brick wall.","proverb"],"魚心あれば水心":["Kindness begets kindness; you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours","It means that if the other person shows goodwill, you'll return it in kind. Literally 'if the fish has a heart for the water, the water has a heart for the fish.'","Kindness begets kindness: if you're kind to others, they'll be kind to you too.","proverb"],"餅は餅屋":["Leave it to the experts","Literally 'for rice cakes, go to the rice cake shop.' It means that every job is best left to the professionals.","Leave it to the experts: let's have a professional fix the computer.","proverb"],"門前の小僧習わぬ経を読む":["You naturally pick things up from your surroundings","Literally 'the boy at the temple gate can recite sutras he was never taught.' It means your environment has a big influence, and you naturally learn what you're constantly exposed to.","Like the temple boy who recites sutras he was never taught, I started understanding a bit of Japanese just from watching Japanese dramas every day.","proverb"],"冬至":["Winter Solstice (one of the 24 solar terms)","One of the 24 solar terms, around December 22, the shortest day of the year. Japanese customs include eating pumpkin and taking a yuzu citron bath (ゆず湯).","On the night of the winter solstice, I warmed up in a yuzu bath.","season"],"住めば都":["Anywhere feels like home once you live there","It means that wherever you live, once you get used to it, it feels as comfortable as the capital. Literally 'live there, and it becomes the capital.'","At first I was uneasy about the countryside, but anywhere feels like home once you live there, and now I really love it.","proverb"],"クリスマスイブ":["Christmas Eve","In Japan it's a day to spend with a romantic partner or family. Eating fried chicken and strawberry shortcake is a uniquely Japanese tradition.","On Christmas Eve, our family gathered around a cake.","season"],"病は気から":["Illness starts in the mind","It means illness is often tied to your state of mind, and a positive mood can help your body get better. Literally 'sickness comes from the spirit.'","Sickness starts in the mind, you know. Cheer up.","proverb"],"三つ子の魂百まで":["The child is father of the man","It means a child's character at age three stays the same until they're a hundred. It's close to English 'The child is father of the man' or 'As the twig is bent, so grows the tree.'","My little brother has hated losing ever since he was small. The soul of a three-year-old lasts until a hundred.","proverb"],"犬も歩けば棒に当たる":["Get out there and you'll stumble onto something","Literally 'a dog that walks around will run into a stick,' it originally meant that meddling leads to trouble. Today it's mostly used to mean that getting out and about can bring unexpected good luck.","Go out and you'll run into something good: I found a lovely café while out on a walk.","proverb"],"仕事納め":["the last workday of the year","The last working day of the year, which for government offices is usually December 28. The first workday of the new year is called 「仕事始め」.","Today is our last day of work for the year. Thank you for all your help this past year.","season"],"備えあれば憂いなし":["Better safe than sorry; forewarned is forearmed","From the Chinese classic Book of Documents, it means that if you prepare in advance, you have nothing to worry about. English says 'Forewarned is forearmed.'","If you're prepared, there's nothing to worry about. Let's buy some water in case of an earthquake.","proverb"],"良いお年を":["Have a happy New Year","A greeting when parting at the end of the year, short for 「良いお年をお迎えください」 (may you welcome a good new year). Once the new year begins it's no longer used, and you switch to 「あけましておめでとう」.","Well then, have a happy New Year! I look forward to seeing you again next year.","season"],"大晦日":["New Year's Eve","The last day of the year. Japanese people eat 「年越しそば」 (year-crossing soba), which symbolizes long life and cutting off the year's bad luck, and listen to temple bells ring 108 times.","On New Year's Eve, we eat year-crossing soba while listening to the temple bells ring out the old year.","season"]};
